WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.301
Put a number into a machine.
Out comes a different number.

2
00:00:08.521 --> 00:00:12.329
What goes in is the input.
What comes out is the output.

3
00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:17.845
Most algorithms start with an input:
the thing they are given to work on.

4
00:00:18.065 --> 00:00:23.312
A recipe's input is the ingredients.
Eggs, flour and sugar go in.

5
00:00:23.532 --> 00:00:27.201
A sum's input is the two
numbers. Give it 3 and 4.

6
00:00:27.421 --> 00:00:31.415
A search's input is the word
you type. You type lions.

7
00:00:31.635 --> 00:00:35.304
Robo's input is the program
of arrows you give it.

8
00:00:35.524 --> 00:00:39.146
The input is not a step. It
is what the steps work on.

9
00:00:39.646 --> 00:00:45.265
An algorithm can be a machine. Input
in one side, output out the other.

10
00:00:45.485 --> 00:00:49.061
In the middle are the steps.
This is the doubling machine.

11
00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:54.250
Take the number in. Add the number
to itself. Send the answer out.

12
00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:58.882
Put 3 in. 3 added to itself.
Out comes 6, every time.

13
00:00:59.102 --> 00:01:02.771
Put 5 in, and the same
steps send 10 out instead.

14
00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:08.797
The output is the result: the
answer, the cake, the search results.

15
00:01:09.017 --> 00:01:14.590
Make a cup of squash. Squash and
water go in: they are the input.

16
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.175
Pour and stir are steps. The
output is the cup at the end.

17
00:01:19.395 --> 00:01:22.832
Same machine, a different
input, a different output.

18
00:01:23.332 --> 00:01:27.651
Once you know the steps, you can work
out an output nobody has shown you.

19
00:01:27.871 --> 00:01:33.536
Here is the add-3 machine. Take the number
in, add 3 to it, send the answer out.

20
00:01:33.756 --> 00:01:38.214
Put 10 in. What comes out?
Follow the steps in your head.

21
00:01:38.434 --> 00:01:42.382
13. You did not have to see
it. That is logical thinking.

22
00:01:42.882 --> 00:01:46.411
Sometimes nobody tells you the
rule. You work it out yourself.

23
00:01:46.631 --> 00:01:49.789
Put 4 in, and 12 comes
out. What is the rule?

24
00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:54.328
Add 8 fits that. Times 3
fits it too. So try another.

25
00:01:54.548 --> 00:01:59.146
Put 5 in, and 15 comes
out. Now add 8 is beaten.

26
00:01:59.366 --> 00:02:03.220
Times 3 survives. One pair
of numbers is never enough.

27
00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:08.689
These machines are linear: no
choices, just one step after another.

28
00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:13.228
Input in. Step, step, step.
Output out. Straight through.

29
00:02:13.448 --> 00:02:18.139
Nothing in the middle asks a question.
Nothing sends you a different way.

30
00:02:18.359 --> 00:02:23.560
Later you will meet algorithms that
ask questions. They take two routes.

31
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.193
The input goes in. The steps
work on it. The output comes out.

32
00:02:28.413 --> 00:02:32.035
The rule is what the machine
does to whatever you give it.

33
00:02:32.255 --> 00:02:36.296
Know the steps, and you can predict
the output before you try it.

34
00:02:36.516 --> 00:02:39.302
Same steps, a new input, a new answer.
